# Notes and ideas for the project

### Notes from the assignment:

Reference paper: understand the problem and possible solution.
Dataset paper: introduces the suggested dataset and explains how it has been created.

### Problem:

What is visual saliency? Typically, humans do not process the entire image uniformly but only
on specific areas.
Visual saliency refers to the regions of an image that naturally attract human attention.

Actually, people look at specific regions, which are then well-focuse, but the area of the
perceived image is way larger than the are we actually see.

This information may be useful for several purposes, e.g., compression. Suppose we want to
compress an image, that is, reducing the number of pixels representing the content without
affecting too much the perceived quality. 

One way to proceed is to quantize more the regione where humans were not focus, while keeping
higher quality in more salient regions. In this way, the final perceived quality remains high
as humans may be focused on salient regions and so they are not affecting by higher degradations
in the rest of the image.

Other applications for streaming purposes: if we want to optimize a streaming pipeline, we can 
send at higher bit-rates more salient portions of the image while reducing the bit-rate for all
the rest of the content.

### Saliency prediction:

One should track human focusing to track saliency information. However, this is costly and
non-feasible in most cases. Then, the objective is to produce a computational model of visual
attention, predicting where humans will look. 

The idea is to map an image to a heatmap (or saliency map) where we define for each pixel the
probability that humans will focus on it:
			f(IMAGE) = HEATMAP
Brighter pixel -> higher probability to be salient pixel

### Dataset:

SALICON dataset contains a large set of saliency annotations on the popular MS COCO: 10k images
for training, 5k for validation and 5k for test.

Problem with this dataset: does not provide ground-truth maps for the test set, but it requires
to upload them to their evaluation server. To avoid this: one may split the validation set into
two subset and use one of them for validation and other for testing. Another solution is to use 
MIT1003 dataset for testing

[https://people.csail.mit.edu/tjudd/WherePeopleLook/index.html](https://people.csail.mit.edu/tjudd/WherePeopleLook/index.html)

### Possible tasks:

- Base: implement a simple saliency prediction model designing a CNN encoder (e.g. ResNet) and
a corresponding decoder using upsampling layers to produce the final map.

- Intermediate: improve the baseline model by introducing architectural modifications inspired by
reference paper (extract features at different depths and fuse them together, add skip 
connections (can be beneficial for this task))

- Advanced: replace the CNN encoder with a lightweight transform-based model and use it for
feature extraction (since it is more advanced, it should allow to have a more complete and 
complex representation of the contant, achieving in principle a better final result).

###Suggestion:

Start from a simple network, it is easier to understand what happens and modfying accordingly.
Then, design more complex ones.

Choose a proper loss function: start with MSE (compare predicted mask with ground-truth one)
and later replace or combine it with other components (for example, predicted probability distr
vs ground-truth one)

Visualize the output: since the goal is to predict where people focus, the results should be
consistent with what one should observe.

